{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "oraclint",
          "version": "v0.0.0-test",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "parse-error",
              "name": "FileCouldNotBeScanned",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "File could not be scanned"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "File could not be scanned. Fix the file so it parses, or exclude it from the scan."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Fix the file so it parses, or exclude it from the scan.",
                "markdown": "# File could not be scanned\n\nFix the file so it parses, or exclude it from the scan."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "invalid-suppression",
              "name": "InvalidSuppressionDirective",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Invalid suppression directive"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Invalid suppression directive. Use \"# oraclint:ignore RULE-ID reason=\\\"why this is acceptable\\\"\" with an existing rule ID."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use \"# oraclint:ignore RULE-ID reason=\\\"why this is acceptable\\\"\" with an existing rule ID.",
                "markdown": "# Invalid suppression directive\n\nUse \"# oraclint:ignore RULE-ID reason=\\\"why this is acceptable\\\"\" with an existing rule ID."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "internal-error",
              "name": "RuleFailed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Rule failed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Rule failed. This is a bug in oraclint; please report it with the oraclint version (do not include secrets)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "This is a bug in oraclint; please report it with the oraclint version (do not include secrets).",
                "markdown": "# Rule failed\n\nThis is a bug in oraclint; please report it with the oraclint version (do not include secrets)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-AUD-001",
              "name": "AuditLogsForwardedOverPlaintext",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Audit logs forwarded over plaintext"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Audit logs forwarded over plaintext. Use an https:// AuditLogger.ForwardToUrl, or forward to a collector on a private network."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use an https:// AuditLogger.ForwardToUrl, or forward to a collector on a private network.",
                "markdown": "# NS-AUD-001: Audit logs forwarded over plaintext\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| low | config | stable | CWE-319 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `AuditLogger.Enabled = true` and `AuditLogger.ForwardToUrl` uses\n`http://` to a non-internal host.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nAudit events record security-relevant operator actions. Sent over plaintext\nthey can be read, and an attacker on the path can drop or alter them, which\nundermines the point of an audit trail. Any `AuditLogger.Headers` (often an\nauthorization token) travel in cleartext too.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[AuditLogger]\nEnabled = true\nForwardToUrl = 'http://audit.example.com/events'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[AuditLogger]\nEnabled = true\nForwardToUrl = 'https://audit.example.com/events'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#auditlogger\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-AUD-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-DB-001",
              "name": "SqlQueryLoggingEnabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL query logging enabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "SQL query logging enabled. Remove Database.LogQueries (default false); use Postgres statement logging on the database side if needed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove Database.LogQueries (default false); use Postgres statement logging on the database side if needed.",
                "markdown": "# NS-DB-001: SQL query logging enabled\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | config | stable | CWE-532 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `Database.LogQueries = true` (default `false`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe node then logs the SQL statements it runs (at debug level). Statements can\ncontain job specs, bridge configuration and other data that should not be\ncopied into log storage. The node documentation itself recommends enabling\nstatement logging on Postgres instead when a full SQL log is needed.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Database]\nLogQueries = true\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Database]\n# LogQueries removed (defaults to false)\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#logqueries\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-DB-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-532"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-DB-002",
              "name": "PostgresConnectionToRemoteHostWithoutTls",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Postgres connection to remote host without TLS"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Postgres connection to remote host without TLS. Use sslmode=verify-full (or at least sslmode=require) for databases reached over a network."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use sslmode=verify-full (or at least sslmode=require) for databases reached over a network.",
                "markdown": "# NS-DB-002: Postgres connection to remote host without TLS\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | secrets | stable | CWE-319 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `Database.URL` or `Database.BackupURL` in `secrets.toml` points to a\nhost on a public network with `sslmode=disable` or `sslmode=allow`.\n\nUnix sockets (`?host=/var/run/postgresql`) and internal hosts (see NS-RPC-001)\nare not flagged.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe node database stores encrypted keys, job specs, pending transactions and\nbridge credentials. The connection also carries the database password. With\n`disable` nothing is encrypted; with `allow` the client only uses TLS if the\nserver insists, so an active attacker can downgrade it. The official guide uses\n`sslmode=disable` for local testing only and says never to do this on a\nproduction node.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Database]\nURL = 'postgresql://chainlink:...@db.example.com:5432/chainlink?sslmode=disable'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Database]\nURL = 'postgresql://chainlink:...@db.example.com:5432/chainlink?sslmode=verify-full\u0026sslrootcert=/certs/ca.pem'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md#database\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/v1/running-a-chainlink-node\n- https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/libpq-ssl.html\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-DB-002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets",
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-DB-003",
              "name": "PostgresTlsDoesNotVerifyTheServer",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Postgres TLS does not verify the server"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Postgres TLS does not verify the server. Use sslmode=verify-full with sslrootcert pointing to the database CA."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use sslmode=verify-full with sslrootcert pointing to the database CA.",
                "markdown": "# NS-DB-003: Postgres TLS does not verify the server\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| low | secrets | experimental | CWE-295 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `Database.URL` or `Database.BackupURL` points to a non-internal host\nand:\n\n- `sslmode` is absent or `prefer`: TLS is attempted but the client silently\n  falls back to plaintext (the Go Postgres driver defaults to `prefer`); or\n- `sslmode=require` without `sslrootcert`: traffic is encrypted but the server\n  certificate is not verified.\n\nExperimental (disabled by default; enable with `--experimental` or\n`--enable NS-DB-003`): many managed databases are reached this way inside\nnetworks the operator trusts, so the finding can be noisy.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nWithout certificate verification, an attacker who can intercept traffic\nbetween the node and the database can impersonate the database, capture the\npassword and read or modify node state.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Database]\nURL = 'postgresql://chainlink:...@db.example.com:5432/chainlink'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Database]\nURL = 'postgresql://chainlink:...@db.example.com:5432/chainlink?sslmode=verify-full\u0026sslrootcert=/certs/db-ca.pem'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md#database\n- https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/libpq-ssl.html\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-DB-003.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets",
                  "CWE-295"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-DB-004",
              "name": "SimpleDatabasePasswordsAllowed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Simple database passwords allowed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Simple database passwords allowed. Remove AllowSimplePasswords / CL_DATABASE_ALLOW_SIMPLE_PASSWORDS and use a database password of at least 16 characters."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove AllowSimplePasswords / CL_DATABASE_ALLOW_SIMPLE_PASSWORDS and use a database password of at least 16 characters.",
                "markdown": "# NS-DB-004: Simple database passwords allowed\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | secrets, env, compose, k8s | stable | CWE-521 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `Database.AllowSimplePasswords = true` in `secrets.toml` (default\n`false`), or when `CL_DATABASE_ALLOW_SIMPLE_PASSWORDS` is set to `true`\n(case-insensitive, as the node reads it) in a dotenv file, a compose service or\na Kubernetes container.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe node normally refuses database URLs whose password is shorter than 16\ncharacters or has surrounding whitespace. This setting skips that check. The\nnode rejects it on production builds (`DatabaseSecrets.validateConfig`), so the\nrule is an **early warning**: it catches the setting before a deployment fails,\nand before a weak database password reaches a non-production build that is\nexposed anyway.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Database]\nAllowSimplePasswords = true\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Database]\n# AllowSimplePasswords removed; database password of 16+ random characters\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md#allowsimplepasswords\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/core/config/toml/types.go\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-DB-004.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets",
                  "env",
                  "compose",
                  "k8s",
                  "CWE-521"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-DKR-001",
              "name": "OperatorUiPortPublishedOnAPublicInterface",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Operator UI port published on a public interface"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Operator UI port published on a public interface. Publish the port on loopback only (\"127.0.0.1:6688:6688\") and use an SSH tunnel or VPN to reach it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Publish the port on loopback only (\"127.0.0.1:6688:6688\") and use an SSH tunnel or VPN to reach it.",
                "markdown": "# NS-DKR-001: Operator UI port published on a public interface\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | compose | stable | CWE-668 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for a compose service running the node (image or service name contains\n`chainlink`) that publishes container port `6688` (default `WebServer.HTTPPort`)\nor `6689` (default `WebServer.TLS.HTTPSPort`) on anything other than a\nloopback address: no host IP (all interfaces), `0.0.0.0`, `::`, or a specific\nnon-loopback IP. Short (`\"6688:6688\"`) and long (`target:`/`host_ip:`) port\nsyntaxes are both understood, including ranges.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe operator UI and API manage jobs, keys and bridges. The node security best\npractices say to keep port 6688 closed to the internet and to reach it through\nan SSH tunnel. Docker publishes ports by inserting its own firewall rules, so a\npublished port is often reachable even when a host firewall such as `ufw` is\nconfigured to block it.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  chainlink:\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n    ports:\n      - \"6688:6688\"\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  chainlink:\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n    ports:\n      - \"127.0.0.1:6688:6688\"   # then: ssh -L 6688:localhost:6688 host\n```\n\n## Limitations\n\nThe rule uses the default ports. If `WebServer.HTTPPort` is changed, the\npublished port is not matched.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/resources/best-security-practices\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/v1/running-a-chainlink-node\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-DKR-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "compose",
                  "CWE-668"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-DKR-002",
              "name": "PostgresPortPublishedToTheHostNetwork",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Postgres port published to the host network"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Postgres port published to the host network. Remove the ports: entry of the database service (the node reaches it over the compose network), or bind it to 127.0.0.1."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove the ports: entry of the database service (the node reaches it over the compose network), or bind it to 127.0.0.1.",
                "markdown": "# NS-DKR-002: Postgres port published to the host network\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | compose | stable | CWE-668 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for a compose service running Postgres (image name `postgres*`,\n`postgis` or `timescaledb`) that publishes container port `5432` on anything\nother than a loopback address.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe node reaches its database over the compose network and needs no published\nport. Publishing it makes the database, which holds the node's encrypted keys\nand all its state, reachable from outside the host and open to password\nguessing. The official Docker guide publishes `5432:5432` for a local test\nsetup and notes that this makes the database accessible outside Docker.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16\n    ports:\n      - \"5432:5432\"\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16\n    # no ports: the node connects to postgres:5432 on the compose network\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/v1/running-a-chainlink-node\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/resources/best-security-practices\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-DKR-002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "compose",
                  "CWE-668"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-DKR-003",
              "name": "PrivilegedContainerOrHostNamespace",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Privileged container or host namespace"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Privileged container or host namespace. Remove privileged, network_mode: host, pid: host, the listed cap_add entries and host mounts of the Docker socket or system directories; the node does not need them."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove privileged, network_mode: host, pid: host, the listed cap_add entries and host mounts of the Docker socket or system directories; the node does not need them.",
                "markdown": "# NS-DKR-003: Privileged container or host namespace\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | compose | stable | CWE-250 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for any compose service with `privileged: true`, `network_mode: host`,\n`pid: host`, `cap_add` containing `ALL`, `SYS_ADMIN`, `NET_ADMIN`,\n`SYS_PTRACE`, `SYS_MODULE` or `DAC_READ_SEARCH` (with or without the `CAP_`\nprefix), or a bind mount (short or long syntax) of a container runtime socket\n(`/var/run/docker.sock`, `containerd.sock`, `crio.sock`) or of a system\ndirectory of the host (`/`, `/proc`, `/sys`, `/etc`, `/root`, `/dev`, `/boot`,\n`/var/lib/docker`, `/var/lib/kubelet`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThese settings remove the isolation between the container and the host.\nMounting the Docker socket is equivalent to root on the host: any process\nthat can reach it can start a privileged container. A\nbug in the node, an external adapter or any other service with them becomes a\nhost compromise, and from there access to every key and secret on the\nmachine. The node needs none of them.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  chainlink:\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n    privileged: true\n    network_mode: host\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  chainlink:\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n    cap_drop: [ALL]\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.docker.com/reference/compose-file/services/\n- https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-DKR-003.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "compose",
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-DKR-004",
              "name": "NodeContainerRunsAsRoot",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Node container runs as root"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Node container runs as root. Remove user: root and fix volume ownership for UID:GID 14933:14933 instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove user: root and fix volume ownership for UID:GID 14933:14933 instead.",
                "markdown": "# NS-DKR-004: Node container runs as root\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | compose | stable | CWE-250 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when a node service sets `user:` to `root` or UID `0` (with or without a\ngroup). A service without `user:` is not flagged: official production images\nalready run as UID:GID `14933:14933` (`core/chainlink.Dockerfile`, v2.69.0).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nRunning as root inside the container gives a compromised node process more\npower to escape the container and to read or change mounted host files. The\nusual reason for `user: root` is a permission error on a bind mount; the node\ndocumentation explains how to align the mount with UID 14933 instead.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  chainlink:\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n    user: root\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  chainlink:\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n    # no user override; chown the data directory to 14933:14933\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/core/chainlink.Dockerfile\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#rootdir\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-DKR-004.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "compose",
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-ENV-001",
              "name": "NodeSecretsInADotenvFile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Node secrets in a dotenv file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Node secrets in a dotenv file. Keep the file out of version control (.gitignore) and outside the repository, or use Docker/Kubernetes secrets."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep the file out of version control (.gitignore) and outside the repository, or use Docker/Kubernetes secrets.",
                "markdown": "# NS-ENV-001: Node secrets in a dotenv file\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | env | stable | CWE-312, CWE-538 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for every non-empty node secret variable in a dotenv file (`.env`,\n`.env.*`, `*.env`) inside the scanned tree: `CL_DATABASE_URL`,\n`CL_DATABASE_BACKUP_URL`, `CL_PASSWORD_KEYSTORE`, `CL_PASSWORD_VRF`,\n`CL_PYROSCOPE_AUTH_TOKEN`, `CL_PROMETHEUS_AUTH_TOKEN`, `CL_THRESHOLD_KEY_SHARE`\nand `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`.\n\nTemplate files ending in `.example`, `.sample`, `.template`, `.dist` or `.tmpl`\nare not scanned by default.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nDocker Compose reads `.env` automatically, so it is a convenient place for\nthe node's secrets, and an easy file to commit or include in an image build\ncontext by accident. If it lives in the repository directory, one `git add .`\npublishes the keystore password.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```sh\n# .env in the repository\nCL_PASSWORD_KEYSTORE=...\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\nKeep the file outside the repository (or at least in `.gitignore` and\n`.dockerignore`), or use Docker secrets / Kubernetes Secrets.\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md\n- https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/use-secrets/\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-ENV-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "env",
                  "CWE-312",
                  "CWE-538"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-ENV-002",
              "name": "NodeSecretAsLiteralEnvironmentValue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Node secret as literal environment value"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Node secret as literal environment value. Reference the value instead: compose secrets: or ${VAR} interpolation, Kubernetes valueFrom.secretKeyRef; rotate the exposed secret."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Reference the value instead: compose secrets: or ${VAR} interpolation, Kubernetes valueFrom.secretKeyRef; rotate the exposed secret.",
                "markdown": "# NS-ENV-002: Node secret as literal environment value\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | compose, k8s | stable | CWE-798, CWE-312 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when a node secret variable (see NS-ENV-001 for the list) is given a\nliteral value:\n\n- in compose `environment:` (map or list syntax), unless the value uses\n  `${VAR}`/`$VAR` interpolation or is passed through from the host (`- VAR`).\n  A reference with a default, `${VAR:-value}`, counts as literal: the default\n  is committed in the file and used whenever `VAR` is unset. `$$` is\n  compose's escape for a literal `$`;\n- in a Kubernetes container `env:` entry with `value:` instead of\n  `valueFrom.secretKeyRef`.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nCompose files and manifests are committed, reviewed in pull requests and\ncopied between environments. A literal secret in them is readable by everyone\nwith repository access, and it also appears in `docker inspect`,\n`kubectl get -o yaml` and CI logs.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nenvironment:\n  CL_PASSWORD_KEYSTORE: my-keystore-password\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\n# compose\nenvironment:\n  CL_PASSWORD_KEYSTORE: ${CL_PASSWORD_KEYSTORE}\n\n# kubernetes\nenv:\n  - name: CL_PASSWORD_KEYSTORE\n    valueFrom:\n      secretKeyRef: {name: chainlink-secrets, key: keystore-password}\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md\n- https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/use-secrets/\n- https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-ENV-002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "compose",
                  "k8s",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "CWE-312"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-FS-001",
              "name": "SecretsFileAccessibleByGroupOrOthers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets file accessible by group or others"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets file accessible by group or others. chmod 600 the secrets file and make it owned by the user that runs the node."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "chmod 600 the secrets file and make it owned by the user that runs the node.",
                "markdown": "# NS-FS-001: Secrets file accessible by group or others\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | secrets | stable | CWE-732 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when a secrets file has any group or other permission bit set\n(`mode \u0026 0o077 != 0`), for example `0644` or `0640`.\n\nPOSIX systems only. On Windows, Go does not expose ACLs as permission bits,\nso the rule does not run there. Merged targets have no single file mode and\nare skipped.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n`secrets.toml` holds the database URL with its password and the keystore\npassword. If other local users or services can read it, a compromise of any of\nthem becomes a compromise of the node's keys. The node itself keeps its root\ndirectory at `0700` for the same reason.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```sh\n$ ls -l secrets.toml\n-rw-r--r-- 1 chainlink chainlink 210 secrets.toml\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```sh\nchmod 600 secrets.toml\nchown chainlink:chainlink secrets.toml\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#rootdir\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-FS-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets",
                  "CWE-732"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-IMG-001",
              "name": "ContainerImageNotPinned",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container image not pinned"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Container image not pinned. Pin an explicit version tag, ideally with a digest: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0@sha256:\u003cdigest\u003e."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pin an explicit version tag, ideally with a digest: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0@sha256:\u003cdigest\u003e.",
                "markdown": "# NS-IMG-001: Container image not pinned\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | compose, k8s | stable | CWE-1357, CWE-494 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for every compose service image and Kubernetes container or init\ncontainer image that has no tag (which means `latest`) or the `latest` tag, and\nno `@sha256:` digest. Interpolated references (`${VERSION}`) are skipped.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nUnpinned images change under you: a restart can pull an untested node\nrelease (with new config defaults or database migrations) or a tampered image.\nA version tag makes deployments reproducible; a digest also protects against a\ntag being moved. The node documentation states that the `latest` tag does not\nwork for the node image.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  chainlink:\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:latest\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  chainlink:\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0@sha256:\u003cdigest\u003e\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16.4\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/v1/running-a-chainlink-node\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-IMG-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "compose",
                  "k8s",
                  "CWE-1357",
                  "CWE-494"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-INS-001",
              "name": "DevelopmentOnlyInsecureFlagEnabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Development-only [Insecure] flag enabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Development-only [Insecure] flag enabled. Remove the [Insecure] section from production configuration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove the [Insecure] section from production configuration.",
                "markdown": "# NS-INS-001: Development-only [Insecure] flag enabled\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | config | stable | CWE-489 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for each of these set to `true` (all default to `false`):\n\n- `Insecure.DevWebServer`: skips secure web server settings (allowed hosts, SSL).\n- `Insecure.OCRDevelopmentMode`: runs OCR in development mode.\n- `Insecure.InfiniteDepthQueries`: disables the GraphQL query depth limit.\n- `Insecure.DisableRateLimiting`: disables rate limiting.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThese switches remove protections of the operator API and OCR to make local\ndevelopment easier. Production builds of the node reject them at startup\n(`Insecure.ValidateConfig`; `OCRDevelopmentMode` is also accepted by test\nbuilds). This rule is an **early warning**: it catches the setting in CI before a\ndeployment fails, and before a development build with these settings is\nexposed.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Insecure]\nDevWebServer = true\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n# no [Insecure] section in production configuration\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#insecure\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/core/config/toml/types.go\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-INS-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-489"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-K8S-001",
              "name": "PrivilegedPodOrHostNamespace",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Privileged pod or host namespace"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Privileged pod or host namespace. Remove hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC, securityContext.privileged and hostPath volumes of runtime sockets or system directories."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC, securityContext.privileged and hostPath volumes of runtime sockets or system directories.",
                "markdown": "# NS-K8S-001: Privileged pod or host namespace\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | k8s | stable | CWE-250 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for workloads (Pod, Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet, ReplicaSet,\nReplicationController, Job, CronJob) whose pod spec sets `hostNetwork`,\n`hostPID` or `hostIPC` to `true`, mounts a `hostPath` volume of a container\nruntime socket or system directory of the node (`/var/run/docker.sock`,\n`containerd.sock`, `crio.sock`, `/`, `/proc`, `/sys`, `/etc`, `/root`, `/dev`,\n`/boot`, `/var/lib/docker`, `/var/lib/kubelet`), and for every container or\ninit container with `securityContext.privileged: true`. Objects inside a\n`kind: List` document are checked too (as for every Kubernetes rule).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThese settings are forbidden by the Kubernetes \"baseline\" Pod Security\nStandard because they break the isolation between the pod and the worker\nnode. A compromised container could read other pods' traffic, processes and\nsecrets on that node.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      hostNetwork: true\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: node\n          image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-K8S-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "k8s",
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-K8S-002",
              "name": "ContainerSecuritycontextNotHardened",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container securityContext not hardened"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Container securityContext not hardened. Set allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, runAsNonRoot: true and readOnlyRootFilesystem: true (mount a writable volume for the node's root directory)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, runAsNonRoot: true and readOnlyRootFilesystem: true (mount a writable volume for the node's root directory).",
                "markdown": "# NS-K8S-002: Container securityContext not hardened\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | k8s | stable | CWE-250 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires once per container or init container that lacks any of:\n\n- `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`;\n- `runAsNonRoot: true` (container level, or inherited from the pod's\n  `securityContext` when the container does not set it);\n- `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true`.\n\nThe message lists exactly which settings are missing. (This rule also covers\nwhat was planned as NS-K8S-003, so that one container yields one finding.)\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThese are the core settings of the Kubernetes \"restricted\" profile. They limit\nwhat an attacker can do after exploiting the container: no setuid escalation,\nno root inside the container, and no persistence by modifying the image's\nfiles. The node writes to its root directory, so mount a writable volume there\nwhen enabling a read-only root filesystem.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\ncontainers:\n  - name: node\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nsecurityContext:\n  runAsNonRoot: true\n  runAsUser: 14933\ncontainers:\n  - name: node\n    image: smartcontract/chainlink:2.69.0\n    securityContext:\n      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/\n- https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/security-context/\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-K8S-002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "k8s",
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-K8S-004",
              "name": "OperatorUiExposedByAKubernetesService",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Operator UI exposed by a Kubernetes Service"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Operator UI exposed by a Kubernetes Service. Use a ClusterIP Service and reach the UI with kubectl port-forward, a VPN or an authenticated ingress restricted to operators."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use a ClusterIP Service and reach the UI with kubectl port-forward, a VPN or an authenticated ingress restricted to operators.",
                "markdown": "# NS-K8S-004: Operator UI exposed by a Kubernetes Service\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | k8s | stable | CWE-668 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for every port of a `Service` of type `LoadBalancer` or `NodePort`\nwhose `port` or numeric `targetPort` is `6688` or `6689` (the node's default\nHTTP and HTTPS ports). Named target ports are not resolved.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n`LoadBalancer` and `NodePort` Services make the port reachable from outside\nthe cluster, usually from the internet. The operator UI and API should only be\nreachable by operators, for example through `kubectl port-forward`, a VPN or\nan authenticated ingress restricted to them.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nkind: Service\nspec:\n  type: LoadBalancer\n  ports:\n    - port: 6688\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nkind: Service\nspec:\n  type: ClusterIP\n  ports:\n    - port: 6688\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/resources/best-security-practices\n- https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-K8S-004.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "k8s",
                  "CWE-668"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-KS-001",
              "name": "KeystoreEncryptedWithInsecureFastScryptParameters",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Keystore encrypted with insecure fast scrypt parameters"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Keystore encrypted with insecure fast scrypt parameters. Remove InsecureFastScrypt (default false) and rotate keys created while it was enabled."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove InsecureFastScrypt (default false) and rotate keys created while it was enabled.",
                "markdown": "# NS-KS-001: Keystore encrypted with insecure fast scrypt parameters\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | config | stable | CWE-916 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `InsecureFastScrypt = true` is set in `config.toml` (default: `false`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe node encrypts its private keys (EVM transmitter keys, OCR and P2P keys) with\na password-derived key using scrypt. `InsecureFastScrypt` replaces the scrypt\nparameters with cheap ones meant for tests. Anyone who obtains the database or a\nkey export can then brute-force the keystore password orders of magnitude\nfaster.\n\nThe node documentation says \"DO NOT ENABLE THIS IN PRODUCTION\", but unlike the\n`[Insecure]` section the setting is **not** rejected by production builds\n(verified in v2.69.0), so nothing stops it from reaching a live node.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\nInsecureFastScrypt = true\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n# InsecureFastScrypt removed (defaults to false)\n```\n\nKeys created while the setting was enabled were encrypted with weak parameters;\nrotate them.\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#insecurefastscrypt\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-KS-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-916"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-KS-002",
              "name": "WeakKeystorePassword",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Weak keystore password"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Weak keystore password. Use a randomly generated password of at least 16 characters without surrounding whitespace, and change it with the node's keystore password change procedure."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use a randomly generated password of at least 16 characters without surrounding whitespace, and change it with the node's keystore password change procedure.",
                "markdown": "# NS-KS-002: Weak keystore password\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | secrets, env, compose, k8s | stable | CWE-521 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `Password.Keystore` or `Password.VRF` in `secrets.toml`, or a\nliteral `CL_PASSWORD_KEYSTORE` / `CL_PASSWORD_VRF` in a dotenv file, compose\nservice or Kubernetes container, fails the node's own complexity rule: at least\n16 characters and no leading or trailing whitespace\n(`utils.VerifyPasswordComplexity`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe keystore password protects the node's private keys, including the keys\nthat sign on-chain transactions and OCR reports. The node checks complexity\nonly when it creates a new, empty keystore; for backwards compatibility it\naccepts any password for an existing keystore\n(`core/cmd/key_store_authenticator.go`, v2.69.0). A node set up with a weak\npassword, or set up before the rule existed, keeps running with it.\n\nThe finding never shows the password or its length.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Password]\nKeystore = 'changeme'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Password]\nKeystore = '\u003cat least 16 random characters\u003e'\n```\n\nChange the password of an existing keystore with the node's password change\nprocedure, not only in this file.\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md#password\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/core/utils/password.go\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-KS-002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets",
                  "env",
                  "compose",
                  "k8s",
                  "CWE-521"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-LOG-001",
              "name": "DebugLogLevel",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Debug log level"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Debug log level. Use Log.Level = 'info' (the default) or higher in production; enable debug temporarily when investigating."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use Log.Level = 'info' (the default) or higher in production; enable debug temporarily when investigating.",
                "markdown": "# NS-LOG-001: Debug log level\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| low | config | stable | CWE-532 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `Log.Level = 'debug'` (default `info`, case-insensitive).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nDebug logs record far more detail about requests, job runs and internal state\nthan production needs. Logs are commonly shipped to third-party systems with\nwider access than the node itself, and the extra volume makes real errors\nharder to spot. `Log.Level` controls console output only; file logging has its\nown settings.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Log]\nLevel = 'debug'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Log]\nLevel = 'info'\nJSONConsole = true\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#log\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-LOG-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-532"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-MET-001",
              "name": "PrometheusMetricsEndpointWithoutAuthentication",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prometheus metrics endpoint without authentication"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prometheus metrics endpoint without authentication. Set Prometheus.AuthToken in secrets.toml (or CL_PROMETHEUS_AUTH_TOKEN) and configure the scraper with the bearer token."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set Prometheus.AuthToken in secrets.toml (or CL_PROMETHEUS_AUTH_TOKEN) and configure the scraper with the bearer token.",
                "markdown": "# NS-MET-001: Prometheus metrics endpoint without authentication\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| low | secrets | experimental | CWE-306 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when a scanned `secrets.toml` does not set `Prometheus.AuthToken`.\n\nExperimental (disabled by default; enable with `--experimental` or\n`--enable NS-MET-001`): the token can also come from the\n`CL_PROMETHEUS_AUTH_TOKEN` environment variable, which a file scan of the\nsecrets file cannot see.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe `/metrics` endpoint shares the operator web server port. Without a token\nthe node serves metrics to anyone who can reach that port\n(`core/web/router.go`: the bearer check is skipped when the token is empty).\nMetrics reveal the jobs the node runs, chains, balances-related gauges and\nsoftware versions, which is useful reconnaissance. Profiling endpoints\n(`/debug/pprof`) are always behind authentication.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n# secrets.toml without a [Prometheus] section\n[Password]\nKeystore = '...'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Prometheus]\nAuthToken = '\u003crandom token\u003e'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md#prometheus\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-MET-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets",
                  "CWE-306"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-RPC-001",
              "name": "RpcEndpointUsesUnencryptedTransport",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RPC endpoint uses unencrypted transport"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "RPC endpoint uses unencrypted transport. Use https:// and wss:// endpoints, or keep the RPC node on a private network."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use https:// and wss:// endpoints, or keep the RPC node on a private network.",
                "markdown": "# NS-RPC-001: RPC endpoint uses unencrypted transport\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | config | stable | CWE-319 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for every `HTTPURL`, `WSURL` or `HTTPURLExtraWrite` of an enabled\n`[[EVM]]` chain that uses `http://` or `ws://` to a host on a public network.\n\nHosts considered internal (not flagged): loopback, private and link-local IP\nranges, single-label names such as Docker Compose or Kubernetes service names\n(`geth`), and internal DNS suffixes (`.svc`, `.cluster.local`, `.internal`,\n`.local`, `.lan`, `.home.arpa`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe node trusts its RPC endpoints for chain state and uses them to broadcast\nsigned transactions. Over plaintext, anyone on the network path can read the\ntraffic, including API keys embedded in the URL, and can tamper with responses\n(for example fake block heads or logs) to influence what the node observes and\nreports.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[[EVM]]\nChainID = '1'\n\n[[EVM.Nodes]]\nName = 'primary'\nWSURL = 'ws://rpc.example.com/ws'\nHTTPURL = 'http://rpc.example.com'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[[EVM.Nodes]]\nName = 'primary'\nWSURL = 'wss://rpc.example.com/ws'\nHTTPURL = 'https://rpc.example.com'\n```\n\n## Limitations\n\nPlaintext traffic inside a private network is not flagged. It is a smaller but\nreal risk; the node security best practices recommend TLS even behind a VPN.\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#evmnodes\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/resources/best-security-practices\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-RPC-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-RPC-002",
              "name": "CredentialEmbeddedInRpcUrl",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Credential embedded in RPC URL"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Credential embedded in RPC URL. Keep RPC URLs in a config file that is not committed or inject them via CL_CONFIG from a secret store; restrict the key at the provider (allowed IPs/origins)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep RPC URLs in a config file that is not committed or inject them via CL_CONFIG from a secret store; restrict the key at the provider (allowed IPs/origins).",
                "markdown": "# NS-RPC-002: Credential embedded in RPC URL\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | config | stable | CWE-798, CWE-312 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for every `HTTPURL`, `WSURL` or `HTTPURLExtraWrite` of an `[[EVM.Nodes]]`\nentry (enabled or not) that carries:\n\n- user info (`https://user:password@host`);\n- a query parameter named like a credential (`key`, `apikey`, `token`,\n  `secret`, `auth`, `signature`, ...); or\n- a path segment that looks like an API key: at least 20 characters of\n  letters, digits, `-` or `_`, including a digit. This matches the formats of\n  common providers such as Infura (`/v3/\u003ckey\u003e`), Alchemy (`/v2/\u003ckey\u003e`) and\n  QuickNode (`/\u003ctoken\u003e/`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nRPC provider keys are billable credentials and, when unrestricted, let anyone\nuse the quota or impersonate the node's traffic. The node has no secrets field\nfor RPC URLs, so they live in config files, which are usually committed,\ncopied into tickets and shared with other teams.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[[EVM.Nodes]]\nName = 'infura'\nHTTPURL = 'https://mainnet.infura.io/v3/\u003ckey\u003e'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\nMove the `[[EVM]]` sections with keys to a separate config file that is not\ncommitted (the node merges multiple `-config` files in order), or inject them\nwith the `CL_CONFIG` environment variable from a secret manager; the node\napplies `CL_CONFIG` last. Restrict keys at the provider to the node's IP\naddresses. Rotate any key that was committed.\n\n## Limitations\n\nKeys shorter than 20 characters in the URL path are not recognized.\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#evmnodes\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-RPC-002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "CWE-312"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-RPC-003",
              "name": "SinglePrimaryRpcNodeForChain",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Single primary RPC node for chain"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Single primary RPC node for chain. Configure at least two primary [[EVM.Nodes]], ideally from different providers, or set IsLoadBalancedRPC = true if the URL fronts several RPC nodes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Configure at least two primary [[EVM.Nodes]], ideally from different providers, or set IsLoadBalancedRPC = true if the URL fronts several RPC nodes.",
                "markdown": "# NS-RPC-003: Single primary RPC node for chain\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| low | config | stable | CWE-1357 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for each enabled `[[EVM]]` chain (`Enabled` defaults to `true`) that has\nexactly one primary node (`SendOnly` not `true`), when that node is not marked\n`IsLoadBalancedRPC = true`.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nAn oracle node is only as available and as honest as its view of the chain.\nWith a single primary RPC endpoint, an outage at that provider stops the node\non that chain, and stale or manipulated responses cannot be noticed by\ncomparing with another source. The node security best practices recommend\nredundant, load-balanced RPC connectivity. With a single non-load-balanced\nnode, the node also marks it alive even when it is out of sync\n(`IsLoadBalancedRPC` docs).\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[[EVM]]\nChainID = '1'\n\n[[EVM.Nodes]]\nName = 'only'\nHTTPURL = 'https://rpc-a.example.com'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[[EVM.Nodes]]\nName = 'provider-a'\nHTTPURL = 'https://rpc-a.example.com'\n\n[[EVM.Nodes]]\nName = 'provider-b'\nHTTPURL = 'https://rpc-b.example.net'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#evmnodes\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/resources/best-security-practices\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-RPC-003.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-1357"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-SEC-001",
              "name": "SecretSettingInConfigFile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret setting in config file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Secret setting in config file. Move the setting to secrets.toml (or its CL_* environment variable) and rotate the exposed secret."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Move the setting to secrets.toml (or its CL_* environment variable) and rotate the exposed secret.",
                "markdown": "# NS-SEC-001: Secret setting in config file\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | config | stable | CWE-260, CWE-312 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when a key that belongs to the secrets schema appears in a config file:\n`Database.URL`, `Database.BackupURL`, `Password.Keystore`, `Password.VRF`,\n`WebServer.LDAP.ReadOnlyUserPass`, `WebServer.OIDC.clientSecret`,\n`Prometheus.AuthToken`, `Pyroscope.AuthToken`, `Mercury.Credentials`,\n`Threshold.ThresholdKeyShare`, `CRE.Streams.ApiKey`, `CRE.Streams.ApiSecret`,\n`CRE.LocalSecretOverrides`, `P2PKey`, `DKGRecipientKey`.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe node keeps configuration and secrets in separate files so the\nconfiguration can be versioned and shared while secrets stay in a secret store.\nA credential in `config.toml` tends to end up in Git history, CI logs and\nsupport tickets.\n\nThe node decodes config files strictly and **refuses to start** with these\nkeys, so this rule is an early warning: by the time the node complains, the\nsecret may already be committed. Treat it as exposed and rotate it.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n# config.toml\n[Password]\nKeystore = '...'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n# secrets.toml (not committed, mode 0600)\n[Password]\nKeystore = '...'\n```\n\nor set `CL_PASSWORD_KEYSTORE` from a secret manager.\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/SECRETS.md\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-SEC-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-260",
                  "CWE-312"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-SEC-002",
              "name": "CredentialInAuditloggerHeaders",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Credential in AuditLogger headers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Credential in AuditLogger headers. Keep the file that sets AuditLogger.Headers out of version control (for example inject it with CL_CONFIG from a secret store) and rotate the credential."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep the file that sets AuditLogger.Headers out of version control (for example inject it with CL_CONFIG from a secret store) and rotate the credential.",
                "markdown": "# NS-SEC-002: Credential in AuditLogger headers\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | config | stable | CWE-260 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires for each `AuditLogger.Headers` entry whose header name looks like a\ncredential (`Authorization`, `*Token*`, `*Api-Key*`, `*Secret*`, `Cookie`,\n`*Password*`) and has a value.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n`AuditLogger.Headers` is part of the config schema and has no `secrets.toml`\nequivalent, so the credential for the audit endpoint ends up in the config\nfile, which is usually committed and shared. Anyone with read access to the\nrepository can then write fake events into, or read from, the audit system.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[AuditLogger]\nHeaders = ['Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken\u003e']\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\nKeep the `[AuditLogger]` section in a separate config file that is not\ncommitted, or pass it through the `CL_CONFIG` environment variable (the node\napplies it last) from a secret manager. Rotate a token that was committed.\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#headers\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-SEC-002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-260"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-TEL-001",
              "name": "TelemetryOverAnInsecureConnection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Telemetry over an insecure connection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Telemetry over an insecure connection. Remove InsecureConnection / ChipIngressInsecureConnection and configure Telemetry.CACertFile."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove InsecureConnection / ChipIngressInsecureConnection and configure Telemetry.CACertFile.",
                "markdown": "# NS-TEL-001: Telemetry over an insecure connection\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | config | stable | CWE-319 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when:\n\n- `Telemetry.Enabled = true` and `Telemetry.InsecureConnection = true` (default\n  `false`) with a non-internal `Telemetry.Endpoint`; or\n- `Telemetry.ChipIngressInsecureConnection = true` (default `false`) with a\n  non-internal `Telemetry.ChipIngressEndpoint`. This applies even when\n  `Telemetry.Enabled` is false: the node's durable emitter\n  (`DurableEmitterEnabled`, default `true`) sends CHIP ingress events on its\n  own (`core/services/chainlink/application.go`, v2.69.0).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nTelemetry and CHIP ingress events carry operational data about the node and\nthe jobs it runs. The documentation says `InsecureConnection` is \"only\navailable in dev mode\", but the node does not enforce this in v2.69.0\n(`Telemetry.ValidateConfig` only skips the `CACertFile` requirement).\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Telemetry]\nEnabled = true\nEndpoint = 'telemetry.example.com:4317'\nInsecureConnection = true\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Telemetry]\nEnabled = true\nEndpoint = 'telemetry.example.com:4317'\nCACertFile = '/certs/telemetry-ca.pem'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#telemetry\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/core/config/toml/types.go\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-TEL-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-TRC-001",
              "name": "TracesExportedUnencryptedToARemoteCollector",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Traces exported unencrypted to a remote collector"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Traces exported unencrypted to a remote collector. Use Tracing.Mode = 'tls' with Tracing.TLSCertPath, or send traces to a collector on the same host or private network."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use Tracing.Mode = 'tls' with Tracing.TLSCertPath, or send traces to a collector on the same host or private network.",
                "markdown": "# NS-TRC-001: Traces exported unencrypted to a remote collector\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | config | stable | CWE-319 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `Tracing.Enabled = true`, `Tracing.Mode = 'unencrypted'` (default\n`tls`) and `Tracing.CollectorTarget` is not a loopback, private or internal\nhost (see NS-RPC-001 for the definition).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nTraces describe what the node is doing (job runs, external calls, timings)\nand are sent continuously. The node intends unencrypted traces only for local\ncollectors (\"Unencrypted traces can not be sent to external networks\"), but in\nv2.69.0 its check (`isValidLocalURI`) only verifies that the target is a\nsyntactically valid `host:port`, so a public collector is accepted.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[Tracing]\nEnabled = true\nMode = 'unencrypted'\nCollectorTarget = 'otel.example.com:4317'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[Tracing]\nEnabled = true\nMode = 'tls'\nCollectorTarget = 'otel.example.com:4317'\nTLSCertPath = '/certs/otel.pem'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#tracing\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/core/config/toml/types.go\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-TRC-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-WEB-001",
              "name": "OperatorUiServedWithoutHttps",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Operator UI served without HTTPS"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Operator UI served without HTTPS. Configure WebServer.TLS (CertPath, KeyPath, HTTPSPort), or terminate TLS at a reverse proxy and suppress this finding with that reason."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Configure WebServer.TLS (CertPath, KeyPath, HTTPSPort), or terminate TLS at a reverse proxy and suppress this finding with that reason.",
                "markdown": "# NS-WEB-001: Operator UI served without HTTPS\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | config | stable | CWE-319 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `WebServer.TLS.HTTPSPort = 0` (default `6689`) while the plaintext\nlistener `WebServer.HTTPPort` is enabled (default `6688`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe operator UI and API control the node: jobs, bridges, keys and\ntransactions. With HTTPS disabled, logins, API credentials and session\ncookies travel in cleartext between the operator and the node. The node runs\nits TLS server only when `HTTPSPort` is non-zero (verified in v2.69.0,\n`core/cmd/shell.go`).\n\nIf TLS is terminated by a reverse proxy in front of the node and the node port\nis not reachable from anywhere else, suppress this finding with that reason.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer.TLS]\nHTTPSPort = 0\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer.TLS]\nCertPath = '/chainlink/tls/server.crt'\nKeyPath = '/chainlink/tls/server.key'\nHTTPSPort = 6689\nForceRedirect = true\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#webservertls\n- https://docs.chain.link/chainlink-nodes/resources/best-security-practices\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-WEB-001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-WEB-002",
              "name": "SessionCookiesWithoutTheSecureFlag",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Session cookies without the Secure flag"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Session cookies without the Secure flag. Remove WebServer.SecureCookies (default true) and access the UI over HTTPS."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove WebServer.SecureCookies (default true) and access the UI over HTTPS.",
                "markdown": "# NS-WEB-002: Session cookies without the Secure flag\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | config | stable | CWE-614 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `WebServer.SecureCookies = false` (default `true`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nWithout the `Secure` attribute, the browser also sends the operator session\ncookie over plaintext HTTP. Anyone able to observe that traffic can replay the\ncookie and act as the operator until the session expires. The node docs\ndescribe this setting as a way to \"enable standard HTTP requests\", i.e. a\ndevelopment convenience.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer]\nSecureCookies = false\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer]\n# SecureCookies removed (defaults to true); access the UI over HTTPS\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#securecookies\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-WEB-002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-614"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-WEB-003",
              "name": "WildcardCorsOrigin",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Wildcard CORS origin"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Wildcard CORS origin. Set WebServer.AllowOrigins to the exact URL(s) used to reach the operator UI."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set WebServer.AllowOrigins to the exact URL(s) used to reach the operator UI.",
                "markdown": "# NS-WEB-003: Wildcard CORS origin\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| medium | config | stable | CWE-942 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when any comma-separated entry of `WebServer.AllowOrigins` is `*`\n(default `http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:6688`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe node passes the entries to gin-contrib/cors (v1.7.2), which switches to\n\"allow all origins\" as soon as one entry is `*` and answers every origin with\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. Browsers do not expose credentialed\nresponses for a wildcard origin, which limits the damage, but any website a\nnode operator visits can still send requests to the node API and read\nunauthenticated responses. The node documentation recommends setting explicit\norigins \"for security reasons\".\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer]\nAllowOrigins = '*'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer]\nAllowOrigins = 'https://node.example.com'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#alloworigins\n- https://github.com/gin-contrib/cors/blob/v1.7.2/config.go\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-WEB-003.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-942"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-WEB-004",
              "name": "HttpNotRedirectedToHttps",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP not redirected to HTTPS"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP not redirected to HTTPS. Set WebServer.TLS.ForceRedirect = true, or disable the plaintext listener with WebServer.HTTPPort = 0."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Set WebServer.TLS.ForceRedirect = true, or disable the plaintext listener with WebServer.HTTPPort = 0.",
                "markdown": "# NS-WEB-004: HTTP not redirected to HTTPS\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| low | config | stable | CWE-319 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when TLS is explicitly configured (`WebServer.TLS.CertPath`, `KeyPath`\nor `HTTPSPort` set, and HTTPS not disabled), the plaintext listener is enabled\n(`WebServer.HTTPPort`, default `6688`) and `WebServer.TLS.ForceRedirect` is not\n`true` (default `false`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nHaving HTTPS available does not help if operators still reach the plaintext\nport: an old bookmark or a typed `http://` URL results in a cleartext login.\n`ForceRedirect` makes the node redirect those requests to HTTPS. Alternatively\ndisable the plaintext listener entirely.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer.TLS]\nCertPath = '/chainlink/tls/server.crt'\nKeyPath = '/chainlink/tls/server.key'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer.TLS]\nCertPath = '/chainlink/tls/server.crt'\nKeyPath = '/chainlink/tls/server.key'\nForceRedirect = true\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#forceredirect\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-WEB-004.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-WEB-005",
              "name": "LongIdleSessionTimeout",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Long idle session timeout"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Long idle session timeout. Keep the session timeout at or below 1h (node default: 15m)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep the session timeout at or below 1h (node default: 15m).",
                "markdown": "# NS-WEB-005: Long idle session timeout\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| low | config | stable | CWE-613 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when an explicitly set idle session timeout is longer than one hour:\n`WebServer.SessionTimeout` (default `15m`), plus `WebServer.LDAP.SessionTimeout`\nor `WebServer.OIDC.SessionTimeout` when that authentication method is active\n(defaults `15m0s`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe idle timeout decides how long a forgotten browser tab or a copied session\ncookie stays usable. Operator sessions can change jobs, bridges and keys, so\nthe node default is short. The one-hour threshold leaves room for convenience\nwhile catching day-long sessions.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer]\nSessionTimeout = '24h'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer]\nSessionTimeout = '15m'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#sessiontimeout\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-WEB-005.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-613"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-WEB-006",
              "name": "LdapAuthenticationWithoutTls",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "LDAP authentication without TLS"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "LDAP authentication without TLS. Remove WebServer.LDAP.ServerTLS = false (default true) and use an ldaps:// server address."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove WebServer.LDAP.ServerTLS = false (default true) and use an ldaps:// server address.",
                "markdown": "# NS-WEB-006: LDAP authentication without TLS\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| high | config | stable | CWE-319, CWE-523 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `WebServer.AuthenticationMethod = 'ldap'` and\n`WebServer.LDAP.ServerTLS = false` (default `true`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nWith LDAP authentication, the node forwards every operator's username and\npassword to the directory server and binds with a read-only service account.\nWithout `ldaps://`, all of these credentials cross the network in cleartext,\nand they are usually corporate credentials valid far beyond the node.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer]\nAuthenticationMethod = 'ldap'\n\n[WebServer.LDAP]\nServerTLS = false\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer]\nAuthenticationMethod = 'ldap'\n\n[WebServer.LDAP]\nServerTLS = true\n```\n\nwith `ServerAddress = 'ldaps://...'` in `secrets.toml`.\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#webserverldap\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-WEB-006.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-319",
                  "CWE-523"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "NS-WEB-007",
              "name": "LoginRateLimitRelaxed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Login rate limit relaxed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Login rate limit relaxed. Keep WebServer.RateLimit.Unauthenticated / UnauthenticatedPeriod near the default (5 per 20s)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep WebServer.RateLimit.Unauthenticated / UnauthenticatedPeriod near the default (5 per 20s).",
                "markdown": "# NS-WEB-007: Login rate limit relaxed\n\n| Severity | Target | Status | CWE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| low | config | experimental | CWE-307 |\n\n## Description\n\nFires when `WebServer.RateLimit.Unauthenticated` and\n`WebServer.RateLimit.UnauthenticatedPeriod` (defaults `5` per `20s`, i.e.\n15 per minute) are set so that more than 30 unauthenticated requests per minute\nare allowed.\n\nExperimental (disabled by default; enable with `--experimental` or\n`--enable NS-WEB-007`): the right limit depends on how many people and tools\nreach the node, so the threshold is a judgment call.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe unauthenticated limit is what throttles password guessing against the\noperator login. Relaxing it makes online brute-force attacks faster.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer.RateLimit]\nUnauthenticated = 100\nUnauthenticatedPeriod = '1m'\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```toml\n[WebServer.RateLimit]\nUnauthenticated = 5\nUnauthenticatedPeriod = '20s'\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/blob/v2.69.0/docs/CONFIG.md#webserverratelimit\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/oraclint/blob/main/docs/rules/NS-WEB-007.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "config",
                  "CWE-307"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        }
